<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Myonessus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/190-bce">190 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-seleucid-war">Roman–Seleucid War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Myonessus was a naval battle fought off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> in 190 BCE, in which a Roman and Rhodian fleet under Lucius Aemilius Regillus defeated the Seleucid fleet commanded by Polyxenidas. The victory gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and allowed their army to cross into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, where it defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> at Magnesia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-myonessus.jpg" alt="Battle of Myonessus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matt Boulton · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhodian_Trireme.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III the Great</a> expanded into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and, in 196 BCE, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where he rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachia">Lysimachia</a>. Rome demanded that he leave Europe and respect the autonomy of the Greek cities of Asia Minor. Encouraged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a>, he landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 192 BCE, but after his defeat at the Battle of Thermopylae on 26 April 191 BCE he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>. To invade Asia Minor the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, and both sides hurried to build ships and draft seamen.</p><p>In September 191 BCE a Roman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamenes">Pergamene</a> fleet defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> at the Battle of Corycus. In spring 190 BCE Polyxenidas, himself a Rhodian exile, destroyed a Rhodian squadron at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> by pretending he would surrender his fleet to its admiral Pausistratos, whom he killed. In August the Rhodians under Eudamus destroyed half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-eurymedon">Battle of the Eurymedon</a>. When Lucius Aemilius Regillus sent part of his fleet to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> in September, Polyxenidas chose to attack.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The allied fleet of 58 Roman and 22 Rhodian warships had sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> for grain and to relieve Notion, besieged by Antiochus. Polyxenidas, with 89 warships and less experienced crews, hid behind the island of Makris to ambush it as it left the narrow northern harbour of Teos, but a Tean peasant warned Aemilius, who moved to the southern harbour.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> attacked as the allies put to sea between Myonessus and the Corycus peninsula. Aemilius formed his line and counterattacked the Seleucid right-centre, exposing his seaward flank. Eudamus led the Rhodians behind the centre to reinforce it, and their fire baskets spread panic, while Aemilius broke through and struck the Seleucid seaward wing from behind. The Seleucids lost 29 ships sunk and 13 captured, the allies two sunk and one captured.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Polyxenidas took his remaining 47 ships to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>. Aemilius told the consul Lucius Cornelius Scipio Asiaticus that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> was secure and sent 30 Roman ships and the Rhodian squadron to help the army cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>, while other Rhodians blockaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> at Megiste. Antiochus withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and offered to pay half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s war costs and accept the demands of 196 BCE, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> pressed on.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Myonessus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
